  "market_commentary": {
    "overview": "",
    "bulkers": "Greek controlled Post Panamax sisters TRINITY I & ROZA (93k dwt built 2010 at \nYZJ/China) were sold together to Chinese buyers for $11.5 million each, matching the price of \nOCEAN VENUS (93k dwt built 2010 at Jinling/China) sold in December.  \nThree kamsarmax vessels changed hands this week: Greek buyers acquired THREE SASKIAS (81k \ndwt built 2014 at JMU/Japan) from the Japanese market for $ 26.8 million, a notable increase \ncompared to the November sale of NEW ASCENT (82k dwt built 2012 at Tsuneishi/Japan) for $19.9 \nmillion. Greek interests also committed the eco main engine AQUAVITA SEA (81k dwt built 2020 at \nHantong/China) for $ 30.5 million, significantly higher than the September sale of a similar vessel, \nCSSC TAI YUAN (81.5k dwt built 2020 Xingang/China), for $ 25 million. Tier-2 vessel \nEPIPHANIA (80k dwt built 2012 at STX/S. Korea) was acquired by European interests for $17.6 mill, \nconsistent with the early February sale of ATHINA CARRAS (82k built 2012 Daewoo/S. Korea) for \n$17.5 million.  \nIn the handy segment, Greek-controlled ADVENTURE (33.7k built 2011 Samjin/China) found buyers \nat a price of $ 9.5 mill with surveys pending, a much softer level compared with sale of sister vessel \nEUROSKY (33.7k dwt built 2011 Samjin/China) for $ 11 million earlier this month.",
    "demolition": "Subcontinent shipping rates remain stable and positive, driven by strong demand for \ntonnage coupled with limited supply. Notably, no vessels have been beached in Pakistan over the past \ntwo weeks. For an overview of demolition market rates, please check our comprehensive demometer \nbelow:"
  },
